an","given":"R."},{"family":"Watson","given":"R."},{"family":"Yahara","given":"T."},{"family":"Hamid","given":"Z. A."},{"family":"Akosim","given":"C."},{"family":"Al-Hafedh","given":"Y."},{"family":"Allahverdiyev","given":"R."},{"family":"Amankwah","given":"E."},{"family":"Asah","given":"S. T."},{"family":"Asfaw","given":"Z."},{"family":"Bartus","given":"G."},{"family":"Brooks","given":"L. A."},{"family":"Caillaux","given":"J."},{"family":"Dalle","given":"G."},{"family":"Darnaedi","given":"D."},{"family":"Driver","given":"A."},{"family":"Erpul","given":"G."},{"family":"Escobar-Eyzaguirre","given":"P."},{"family":"Failler","given":"P."},{"family":"Fouda","given":"A. M. M."},{"family":"Fu","given":"B."},{"family":"Gundimeda","given":"H."},{"family":"Hashimoto","given":"S."},{"family":"Homer","given":"F."},{"family":"Lavorel","given":"S."},{"family":"Lichtenstein","given":"G."},{"family":"Mala","given":"W. A."},{"family":"Mandivenyi","given":"W."},{"family":"Matczak","given":"P."},{"family":"Mbizvo","given":"C."},{"family":"Mehrdadi","given":"M."},{"family":"Metzger","given":"J. P."},{"family":"Mikissa","given":"J. B."},{"family":"Moller","given":"H."},{"family":"Mooney","given":"H. A."},{"family":"Mumby","given":"P."},{"family":"Nagendra","given":"H."},{"family":"Nesshover","given":"C."},{"family":"Oteng-Yeboah","given":"A. A."},{"family":"Pataki","given":"G."},{"family":"Roué","given":"M."},{"family":"Rubis","given":"J."},{"family":"Schultz","given":"M."},{"family":"Smith","given":"P."},{"family":"Sumaila","given":"R."},{"family":"Takeuchi","given":"K."},{"family":"Thomas","given":"S."},{"family":"Verma","given":"M."},{"family":"Yeo-Chang","given":"Y."},{"family":"Zlatanova","given":"D."}],"issued":{"date-parts":[["2015"]]}}}],"schema":"https://github.com/citation-style-language/schema/raw/master/csl-citation.json"} and abundance of the method families in the valuation literature5. 
While the method families intuitively map onto the IPBES conceptual framework of human-nature relations (Figure 3.7), it should be noted that the classification of methods families is not meant to be an intercultural nor a multi-worldview approach. It is a pragmatic approach that has been conceived for this specific assessment process. It is heavily informed by a western science worldview that is founded on the notion that values are sourced from a limited set of places (the environment and humans) and that methods can indeed be classified into distinct yet overlapping groups. The method family classification does not account for worldviews that consider additional value sources such as ancestors and other non-human entities, as is the case in many IPLC contexts. In this vein, the method families do not properly include IPLC practices and methods of valuation.
